WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.596
This work,

00:00:04.676 --> 00:00:08.771
presented at NeurIPS 2022 by Johan Larsson and
Jonas Wallin of Lund University,

00:00:08.851 --> 00:00:12.947
introduces the Hessian Screening Rule,

00:00:13.027 --> 00:00:17.122
a new way to speed up fitting the lasso
regularization path.

00:00:17.202 --> 00:00:21.298
Screening rules discard predictors before a model
is fit, shrinking the problem.

00:00:21.378 --> 00:00:25.473
The authors show that using second-order Hessian
information yields both far tighter screening

00:00:25.553 --> 00:00:29.649
and much more accurate warm starts,

00:00:29.729 --> 00:00:33.824
especially when predictors are highly correlated,
where existing rules struggle most.

00:00:33.904 --> 00:00:38.000
The result is a method that is the fastest across
nearly every simulated and real benchmark they tested.

00:00:38.380 --> 00:00:42.943
Sparse regression with the lasso is a workhorse for
high-dimensional data,

00:00:43.023 --> 00:00:44.700
but fitting it is expensive.

00:00:44.780 --> 00:00:47.962
The best penalty strength is never known in advance,

00:00:48.042 --> 00:00:54.361
so practitioners fit an entire path of models
across many penalty values and tune by cross-validation,

00:00:54.441 --> 00:00:55.993
refitting again and again.

00:00:56.073 --> 00:01:00.636
Screening rules help by discarding predictors
before the solver even runs,

00:01:00.716 --> 00:01:02.267
shrinking each subproblem.

00:01:02.347 --> 00:01:07.725
The trouble is that the widely used rules become
conservative and inefficient precisely

00:01:07.805 --> 00:01:10.235
when predictors are strongly correlated,

00:01:10.315 --> 00:01:13.560
which is exactly the regime where speed matters most.

00:01:13.940 --> 00:01:19.676
The authors start from a unifying observation: the
popular strong rule and the working-set

00:01:19.756 --> 00:01:23.553
strategy can both be expressed as estimates of the
gradient,

00:01:23.633 --> 00:01:26.460
or correlation, at the next step of the path.

00:01:26.540 --> 00:01:31.242
When they lean only on first-order information,
these estimates are crude,

00:01:31.322 --> 00:01:33.439
especially under high correlation.

00:01:33.519 --> 00:01:35.313
That crudeness has two costs.

00:01:35.393 --> 00:01:40.160
Screening becomes conservative, keeping far more
predictors than necessary,

00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:44.230
and the warm starts that seed each optimization are
inaccurate,

00:01:44.310 --> 00:01:47.397
so the solver needs many more passes to converge.

00:01:47.477 --> 00:01:52.760
Both problems point to the same fix, richer
curvature information from the Hessian.

00:01:53.140 --> 00:01:56.474
Their contribution is the Hessian Screening Rule.

00:01:56.554 --> 00:01:59.888
It exploits second-order information in two
complementary ways.

00:01:59.968 --> 00:02:03.302
First,

00:02:03.382 --> 00:02:06.717
the Hessian gives a sharper estimate of the
correlation at the next penalty value,

00:02:06.797 --> 00:02:10.131
which translates into far tighter screening.

00:02:10.211 --> 00:02:13.545
Second,

00:02:13.625 --> 00:02:16.959
the same Hessian and its inverse yield a warm start
that is nearly the exact solution

00:02:17.039 --> 00:02:20.373
whenever the active set does not change,

00:02:20.453 --> 00:02:23.787
cutting the number of solver passes dramatically.

00:02:23.867 --> 00:02:27.202
The authors also show how to update the Hessian and
its inverse efficiently as the active set changes,

00:02:27.282 --> 00:02:30.616
extend the method to general smooth convex losses
like logistic regression,

00:02:30.696 --> 00:02:34.030
and release a full C++ and R implementation.

00:02:34.410 --> 00:02:38.390
The method rests on a simple fact: on any interval
where the active set of nonzero coefficients is unchanged,

00:02:38.470 --> 00:02:42.450
the lasso solution is a linear function of the penalty λ.

00:02:42.530 --> 00:02:46.510
That linearity lets the authors write down a
second-order estimate of the correlation

00:02:46.590 --> 00:02:50.570
at the next penalty value using the Hessian of the
active predictors.

00:02:50.650 --> 00:02:54.630
To keep it cheap,

00:02:54.710 --> 00:02:58.690
they restrict the expensive inner products to the
strong-rule set and add a small fraction

00:02:58.770 --> 00:03:02.750
of the unit bound as a safety margin.

00:03:02.830 --> 00:03:06.810
The very same Hessian inverse provides the warm start,

00:03:06.890 --> 00:03:10.870
which is exact when the active set does not change,

00:03:10.950 --> 00:03:14.930
so the solver often converges in a single pass.

00:03:15.010 --> 00:03:18.990
Efficient low-rank updates keep the Hessian and its
inverse current as predictors enter and leave,

00:03:19.070 --> 00:03:23.050
and an approximate-homotopy scheme adaptively
chooses the penalty grid.

00:03:23.430 --> 00:03:26.674
The experiments cover both simulated and real data.

00:03:26.754 --> 00:03:28.629
On simulated Gaussian designs,

00:03:28.709 --> 00:03:34.559
they sweep a low-dimensional regime with ten
thousand observations and a hundred predictors

00:03:34.639 --> 00:03:40.489
and a high-dimensional regime with four hundred
observations and forty thousand predictors,

00:03:40.569 --> 00:03:44.920
each at three correlation levels, zero, point four,
and point eight,

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:47.071
averaged over twenty repetitions.

00:03:47.151 --> 00:03:53.522
They then test twelve real data sets for both
ℓ1-regularized least-squares and logistic regression,

00:03:53.602 --> 00:04:00.625
ranging from small gene-expression matrices up to
problems with millions of features such as news20 and rcv1.

00:04:00.705 --> 00:04:04.340
Baselines are the working-set strategy, Celer, and Blitz.

00:04:04.720 --> 00:04:06.306
The results are decisive.

00:04:06.386 --> 00:04:11.369
Across every simulated configuration, the Hessian
rule takes the least time,

00:04:11.449 --> 00:04:15.632
and its advantage is largest exactly where
competitors struggle,

00:04:15.712 --> 00:04:18.697
the high-correlation, low-dimensional setting.

00:04:18.777 --> 00:04:21.828
On real data it wins on nearly all twelve sets.

00:04:21.908 --> 00:04:25.759
For ℓ1-regularized least-squares it is fastest on
all five,

00:04:25.839 --> 00:04:31.288
and in all but one case it finishes in under half
the time of the next-best method,

00:04:31.368 --> 00:04:32.954
the working-set strategy.

00:04:33.034 --> 00:04:40.682
On YearPredictionMSD it fits the full path in
seventy-nine seconds against five hundred forty-one for the runner-up,

00:04:40.762 --> 00:04:45.412
and on e2006-tfidf in fourteen seconds against one
hundred forty-three,

00:04:45.492 --> 00:04:48.010
speedups of roughly seven to ten times.

00:04:48.390 --> 00:04:51.685
Two component studies show where the gains come from.

00:04:51.765 --> 00:04:54.168
Looking at the warm start in isolation,

00:04:54.248 --> 00:05:01.236
on colon-cancer and YearPredictionMSD the Hessian
warm start collapses the number of coordinate-descent passes,

00:05:01.316 --> 00:05:03.592
frequently to a single pass per step,

00:05:03.672 --> 00:05:09.513
because when the active set does not change the
warm start is essentially the exact solution.

00:05:09.593 --> 00:05:11.678
Looking at screening in isolation,

00:05:11.758 --> 00:05:17.536
the Hessian rule keeps the number of retained
predictors close to the true active-set floor,

00:05:17.616 --> 00:05:21.994
while alternatives like Celer, Blitz, the strong
rule, EDPP, Gap Safe,

00:05:22.074 --> 00:05:25.368
and Sasvi retain orders of magnitude more predictors,

00:05:25.448 --> 00:05:28.170
and the gap widens as correlation increases.

00:05:28.550 --> 00:05:33.181
The takeaway is that a single idea, reusing
second-order Hessian information,

00:05:33.261 --> 00:05:34.404
pays off twice over.

00:05:34.484 --> 00:05:38.197
It tightens screening so the solver sees far fewer
predictors,

00:05:38.277 --> 00:05:43.214
and it supplies warm starts so accurate that many
path steps converge in one pass.

00:05:43.294 --> 00:05:48.536
Together these make the Hessian Screening Rule the
fastest method for fitting lasso and

00:05:48.616 --> 00:05:52.818
ℓ1-regularized logistic regression paths across the
benchmarks tested,

00:05:52.898 --> 00:05:59.670
with the biggest edge in the high-correlation
regime that has historically been the hardest for screening rules.
